window.docContents[221].resolve({"/Lists/#Logical-Foundations--Lists___-Working-with-Structured-Data--Lists-of-Numbers--Type-Classes-and-Overloading--Head-and-Tail":{"contents":"\n\ndef hd (default : Nat) (l : NatList) : Nat :=\n  match l with\n  | [] => default\n  | h :: _ => h\n\n\nBasic theorems about how hd behaves:\n\ntheorem hd_cons h x (t : NatList) : (h :: t).hd x = h := by rfl\n\ntheorem hd_nil x : [].hd x = x := by rfl\n\ndef tl (l : NatList) : NatList :=\n  match l with\n  | [] => []\n  | _ :: t => t\n\n\nBasic theorems about how tl behaves:\n\ntheorem tl_cons h (t : NatList) : (h :: t).tl = t := by rfl\n\ntheorem tl_nil : [].tl = [] := by rfl\n\nexample : hd 0 [1, 2, 3] = 1 := by rw [hd_cons]\nexample : hd 0 [] = 0 := by rw [hd_nil]\nexample : [1, 2, 3].tl = [2, 3] := by rw [tl_cons]\n\n\nWhat does the following function do?def foo (n : Nat) : NatList :=\n  match n with\n  | 0 => []\n  | n' + 1 => (n' + 1) :: foo n'\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Lists: Working with Structured Data\u0009Lists of Numbers\u0009Type Classes and Overloading","header":"4.2.3.1. Head and Tail","id":"/Lists/#Logical-Foundations--Lists___-Working-with-Structured-Data--Lists-of-Numbers--Type-Classes-and-Overloading--Head-and-Tail"}});